[0.061, "o", "\u001b[?25l\u001b[H\u001b[48;2;55;55;55m  \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mplanets; politically banned by nuclear treaties.\u001b[39m                                                                      \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mNuclear Salt-Water Rocket (NSWR)    1–2g for hours (continuously detonating uranium-salt water in an open magnetic \u001b[39m   \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mnozzle)    Moderate (engineering nightmare, no new physics needed)    Exhaust is an intensely radioactive plasma jet;\u001b[39m \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mone fuel tank leak destroys the ship.\u001b[39m                                                                                 \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mMagneto-Inertial / Pulsed Fusion    0.1–1g bursts (magnetic compression of deuterium-tritium or helium-3 pellets)    \u001b[39m \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mModerate to High (likely for interplanetary cargo by the 2100s)    Better suited to steady 0.05g–0.2g burns rather \u001b[39m   \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mthan continuous 1–2g \"grav-drive\" cruising.\u001b[39m                                                                           \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mAntimatter-Catalyzed / Beam-Core    1–2g continuous (closest real-world equivalent to The Expanse's Epstein Drive)   \u001b[39m \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mVery Low within 150 years    Producing and storing tons of antimatter, plus radiating terawatts of heat, remains far\u001b[39m  \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mbeyond 22nd-century engineering.\u001b[39m                                                                                      \r\u001b[1B                                                                                                                        \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mT\u001b[5Ge Realistic 150-Year Outc\u001b[31Gme\u001b[39m                                                                                \r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B                                                                                                                   \r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153mBy the late 2100s, a corsair-sized ca\u001b[41Ggo ship wo\u001b[52G'\u001b[54G cruise a\u001b[65G1g for\u001b[72Gweeks at a time. Ins\u001b[93Ge\u001b[95Gd, it\u001b[101Gwou\u001b[105Gd\u001b[107Glikely cruise\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1Be\u001b[5Gficiently\u001b[16Gt 0.05g to 0.2g using a pulsed fusion drive\u001b[60G(still cutting a\u001b[77GMars trip down to a\u001b[97Gc\u001b[99Guple of weeks), \u001b[39m    \r\u001b[3C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153mhil\u001b[8G keeping an aux\u001b[24Gliary chemical or nuc\u001b[46Gear thermal thruster in reserve for short 1–2g combat or docking burns.\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1BWould you like to calculate travel times to Mars or Jupiter under a 0.1g vs. 1g burn, or explore how ship radiator \r\u001b[2C\u001b[1Bdesigns and magnetic nozzles deal with teraw\u001b[48Gtt-level heat?\u001b[39m                                                          \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mShow Code\u001b[39m                                                                                                             \r\u001b[2C\u001b[5B\u001b[49m\u001b[K\u001b[27;1H\u001b[24;3H\u001b[?25h"]
